WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.369 --> 00:00:01.335
- Hello, everyone!

00:00:01.335 --> 00:00:03.792
So, welcome to another
episode of Unlocking Lily.

00:00:03.792 --> 00:00:07.418
It's always been my dream
to be a speaker, and I quit

00:00:07.418 --> 00:00:11.585
my day job in order to work and
mentor under Evan full time,

00:00:12.787 --> 00:00:16.954
and all of you are coming
along for the ride with me.

00:00:17.963 --> 00:00:20.843
(whoosh)

00:00:20.843 --> 00:00:24.396
So in every episode, I take
on a different challenge

00:00:24.396 --> 00:00:28.363
that really stretch my
comfort zone, all in order

00:00:28.363 --> 00:00:30.654
to help me grow as a speaker.

00:00:30.654 --> 00:00:34.421
So, my challenge this time
is to take a deep dive

00:00:34.421 --> 00:00:38.407
into the three speakers that
I resonated with the most.

00:00:38.407 --> 00:00:39.892
So, they are Tony Robbins,

00:00:39.892 --> 00:00:42.295
Brene Brown and Simon Sinek.

00:00:42.295 --> 00:00:44.962
I'm going to try to
understand, what are the things

00:00:44.962 --> 00:00:47.928
that they did to get to where
they are today, as well as

00:00:47.928 --> 00:00:51.892
the important lessons that I
learned from each one of them.

00:00:51.892 --> 00:00:53.883
Number one is Tony Robbins.

00:00:53.883 --> 00:00:57.617
He's an author, speaker, and
a peak performance coach.

00:00:57.617 --> 00:01:00.034
(rock music)

00:01:06.654 --> 00:01:10.155
So, when Tony Robbins was only
17 years old, he met a man

00:01:10.155 --> 00:01:13.782
by the name of Jim Rohn, who
was a motivational speaker.

00:01:13.782 --> 00:01:15.687
So when Tony Robbins first got started,

00:01:15.687 --> 00:01:19.186
he didn't try to figure
out everything on his own.

00:01:19.186 --> 00:01:22.442
Instead, he went and mentored
under Jim Rohn to see

00:01:22.442 --> 00:01:25.652
what made Jim so successful,
so that way he could apply

00:01:25.652 --> 00:01:30.529
the same actions and same
behavior to himself as well.

00:01:30.529 --> 00:01:32.235
- Some of the things Mr. Shoaff taught me,

00:01:32.235 --> 00:01:33.571
starting at age 25--

00:01:33.571 --> 00:01:37.713
- I had a dear, dear friend
and mentor named Jim Rohn,

00:01:37.713 --> 00:01:40.875
who was my teacher when
I was 17 years old.

00:01:40.875 --> 00:01:43.133
He's somebody, I sent out
some videos, I'll probably

00:01:43.133 --> 00:01:45.516
attach some things to this
video, another one about him,

00:01:45.516 --> 00:01:48.417
just to honor him and his
teachings, but he gave me a way

00:01:48.417 --> 00:01:51.065
of looking at life that
allowed me to not ask life

00:01:51.065 --> 00:01:54.229
to be easier, but to ask that I be better.

00:01:54.229 --> 00:01:57.983
He got me to realize that the
secret to life was to work

00:01:57.983 --> 00:02:00.702
harder on myself than
my job or anything else,

00:02:00.702 --> 00:02:02.651
because then I'd have
something to give people.

00:02:02.651 --> 00:02:05.270
And he really shaped me,
and he just passed away,

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:08.258
and I was thinking, wow, I'm
going to be 49 years old,

00:02:08.258 --> 00:02:10.640
I met him when I was 17.

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:13.751
Think of the fact that my
journey is just beginning

00:02:13.751 --> 00:02:16.428
and think of all the impact
that a just couple ideas had

00:02:16.428 --> 00:02:18.747
on my life, and I want to
really continue to do that

00:02:18.747 --> 00:02:20.718
for you and anybody you care about, so--

00:02:20.718 --> 00:02:23.796
- So, after working and
mentoring under Jim Rohn,

00:02:23.796 --> 00:02:27.103
Tony Robbins went on to doing
his own seminars as well.

00:02:27.103 --> 00:02:30.374
But he didn't get his actual
breakthrough until he cured

00:02:30.374 --> 00:02:34.541
a woman from her lifelong
snake phobia live on stage.

00:02:35.402 --> 00:02:37.091
- There's a famous story about
something, I've forgotten

00:02:37.091 --> 00:02:39.292
now, but somebody had some
kind of phobia, what was it?

00:02:39.292 --> 00:02:40.876
- [Tony] Snake phobia.
- Snake phobia.

00:02:40.876 --> 00:02:42.545
(speaking over each-other)

00:02:42.545 --> 00:02:43.916
- [Interviewer] --seven years,
and you did it in 15 minutes.

00:02:43.916 --> 00:02:46.017
- Yeah, but what made my
career was that I was attacked

00:02:46.017 --> 00:02:48.197
by the psychiatrists on the
radio saying you're a liar,

00:02:48.197 --> 00:02:50.476
you're charlatan, people like
you should not be allowed

00:02:50.476 --> 00:02:53.341
on the radio, you can't do this,
and so, I was a real rebel,

00:02:53.341 --> 00:02:55.383
frankly, and I was, like, getting
in his face, saying great,

00:02:55.383 --> 00:02:56.434
are you a scientist?

00:02:56.434 --> 00:02:58.084
If you are, then test your hypothesis.

00:02:58.084 --> 00:02:59.953
Give you me your patient,
I'll be at the Holiday Inn

00:02:59.953 --> 00:03:01.293
tomorrow night, so it
was like, this shootout,

00:03:01.293 --> 00:03:02.608
and it was like a movie.

00:03:02.608 --> 00:03:04.420
He brought her, I wiped it
out, I wrapped the snake

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:06.539
around her, and a phobia is
an uncontrollable physical

00:03:06.539 --> 00:03:08.458
response to some stimulus.

00:03:08.458 --> 00:03:10.873
Y'know, it's not just fear,
it's shaking, spitting.

00:03:10.873 --> 00:03:12.652
At the end, she was totally
fine, he worked on her

00:03:12.652 --> 00:03:14.675
for seven years, so that
became my signature,

00:03:14.675 --> 00:03:17.141
was to challenge, and then
gradually, I grew, because

00:03:17.141 --> 00:03:19.861
I was really just trying to
prove to everybody, and myself

00:03:19.861 --> 00:03:23.094
as well, that I was worthy,
rather than, y'know, maturing

00:03:23.094 --> 00:03:25.085
a little bit and just knowing
that there are skill sets

00:03:25.085 --> 00:03:26.458
that are valuable for everybody.

00:03:26.458 --> 00:03:30.388
- So, what Tony Robbins taught
me is that success clues.

00:03:30.388 --> 00:03:32.579
Similar to what Tony
Robbins did with Jim Rohn

00:03:32.579 --> 00:03:36.830
is what I am doing with
Evan, which is why I quit

00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:41.663
my very safe, very comfortable
well-paying day job

00:03:41.663 --> 00:03:44.013
to spend full-time hours with Evan.

00:03:44.013 --> 00:03:47.675
Even though a lot of our
days are spent not talking

00:03:47.675 --> 00:03:50.375
to each-other because we're
working on separate projects,

00:03:50.375 --> 00:03:54.930
what I do is I actually study
everything that Evan does.

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:56.919
How does he set up his day?

00:03:56.919 --> 00:03:58.768
How does he interact with people?

00:03:58.768 --> 00:03:59.976
How does he problem-solve?

00:03:59.976 --> 00:04:01.805
And everything else in-between.

00:04:01.805 --> 00:04:04.614
Because I'm trying to
look for clues, myself,

00:04:04.614 --> 00:04:08.027
and patterns and behaviors,
so that way, I can apply

00:04:08.027 --> 00:04:10.937
all of that to myself and
achieve the similar kind

00:04:10.937 --> 00:04:13.030
of success that Even has, too.

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:15.372
So, number two is Brene Brown.

00:04:15.372 --> 00:04:18.907
She is an author, speaker,
and a research professor.

00:04:18.907 --> 00:04:21.431
So when Brene Brown
started out on her journey

00:04:21.431 --> 00:04:24.438
as a researcher, she quickly
realized the importance

00:04:24.438 --> 00:04:27.425
of having the courage
to show up and be seen.

00:04:27.425 --> 00:04:28.846
- Vulnerability is not about winning,

00:04:28.846 --> 00:04:30.674
it's not about losing, it's
about having the courage

00:04:30.674 --> 00:04:32.255
to show up and be seen.

00:04:32.255 --> 00:04:35.255
It's about willingness to say, look,

00:04:36.229 --> 00:04:37.708
I don't have all the answers.

00:04:37.708 --> 00:04:40.090
This is daring greatly for me.

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:42.423
I'm wearing false eyelashes.

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:48.407
And my biggest fear, when
they put 'em on, was like,

00:04:48.407 --> 00:04:51.596
am I going to get halfway
through this thing and be like,

00:04:51.596 --> 00:04:53.501
like I can't do it?

00:04:53.501 --> 00:04:55.970
- If you do, just rip the whole thing off.

00:04:55.970 --> 00:04:57.392
That's happened to me before.

00:04:57.392 --> 00:04:59.117
Just rip 'em both off and there you are.

00:04:59.117 --> 00:05:00.272
Naked before the world.

00:05:00.272 --> 00:05:02.679
- [Brene] You may see it here today.

00:05:02.679 --> 00:05:07.265
Yeah, so, I mean, I think,
people tell me all the time,

00:05:07.265 --> 00:05:09.392
I don't want to be vulnerable,
I don't want to do this,

00:05:09.392 --> 00:05:11.576
'cause I can't bear this.

00:05:11.576 --> 00:05:14.100
And I think being
vulnerable feels dangerous,

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:18.687
and I think it feels scary,
and I think it is terrifying,

00:05:18.687 --> 00:05:21.100
but I don't think it's as
dangerous, scary or terrifying

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:23.970
as getting to the end of
our lives and wondering,

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:27.241
what if I would have shown up?

00:05:27.241 --> 00:05:30.462
What if I would've said, I love you back?

00:05:30.462 --> 00:05:31.685
- [Oprah] Yeah.

00:05:31.685 --> 00:05:33.957
- So that, to me, is
what daring greatly is.

00:05:33.957 --> 00:05:36.678
- So Brene Brown continued to
teach, as well as do research

00:05:36.678 --> 00:05:38.488
at the University of Houston.

00:05:38.488 --> 00:05:41.285
But it wasn't until her popular TEDx talk,

00:05:41.285 --> 00:05:44.003
The Power of Vulnerability,
that received over 30 million

00:05:44.003 --> 00:05:48.170
views, that catapulted her into
the star that she is today.

00:05:49.123 --> 00:05:53.801
- What underpinned this shame,
this I'm not good enough,

00:05:53.801 --> 00:05:56.850
which, we all know that
feeling, I'm not blank enough.

00:05:56.850 --> 00:05:58.294
I'm not think enough, rich
enough, beautiful enough,

00:05:58.294 --> 00:06:00.910
smart enough, promoted enough.

00:06:00.910 --> 00:06:04.857
The thing that underpinned
this was excruciating

00:06:04.857 --> 00:06:09.024
vulnerability, this idea
of, in order for connection

00:06:10.025 --> 00:06:13.582
to happen, we have to
allow ourselves to be seen,

00:06:13.582 --> 00:06:14.582
really seen.

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:17.991
And you know how I feel
about vulnerability,

00:06:17.991 --> 00:06:20.709
I hate vulnerability, and so,
I thought, this is my chance

00:06:20.709 --> 00:06:23.692
to beat it back with my measuring stick.

00:06:23.692 --> 00:06:26.679
I'm going in, I'm going
to figure this stuff out,

00:06:26.679 --> 00:06:29.681
I'm going to spend a year, I'm
going to totally deconstruct shame,

00:06:29.681 --> 00:06:31.510
I'm going to understand
how vulnerability works,

00:06:31.510 --> 00:06:33.677
and I'm going to outsmart it.

00:06:34.588 --> 00:06:38.005
So I was ready, and I was really excited.

00:06:40.231 --> 00:06:42.392
As you know, it's not
going to turn out well.

00:06:42.392 --> 00:06:45.145
(audience laughs)

00:06:45.145 --> 00:06:46.054
You know this.

00:06:46.054 --> 00:06:48.721
- So what Brene Brown taught
me is that I need to have

00:06:48.721 --> 00:06:50.602
the courage to show up.

00:06:50.602 --> 00:06:53.323
There's so many things
that I'm afraid to do,

00:06:53.323 --> 00:06:56.274
because I'm concerned that
people are going to judge me

00:06:56.274 --> 00:07:00.441
and criticize me, but the reality
is, I want to be a speaker.

00:07:01.892 --> 00:07:04.894
I want to be able to inspire
people through my work,

00:07:04.894 --> 00:07:09.164
which means I need to get
outside of my comfort zone

00:07:09.164 --> 00:07:12.664
and do the brave things and be courageous.

00:07:13.752 --> 00:07:16.627
So what I'm going to do is I'm
going to go into the biggest

00:07:16.627 --> 00:07:20.697
intersection in Toronto,
Yonge-Dundas Square,

00:07:20.697 --> 00:07:23.197
and film in front of everyone.

00:07:24.718 --> 00:07:28.662
Even though I am so scared even
thinking about it to do it.

00:07:28.662 --> 00:07:32.007
But I need to do it
because I need to be brave

00:07:32.007 --> 00:07:35.350
and have the courage to show up.

00:07:35.350 --> 00:07:37.915
Number three is Simon Sinek.

00:07:37.915 --> 00:07:41.271
He's an author, leadership
guru and a speaker.

00:07:41.271 --> 00:07:43.720
So, Simon Sinek first
started out by owning his own

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:48.327
business, but he completely
lost passion for what he did,

00:07:48.327 --> 00:07:52.221
which caused him to look
inward and to find his why.

00:07:52.221 --> 00:07:54.141
- And I realized, I knew what I did.

00:07:54.141 --> 00:07:55.101
I was good at it.

00:07:55.101 --> 00:07:57.343
And I knew how I did it,
I could tell you what was

00:07:57.343 --> 00:07:59.807
different or special about
the way I did things.

00:07:59.807 --> 00:08:01.640
But I couldn't tell
you why I was doing it.

00:08:01.640 --> 00:08:04.068
That was the missing piece,
you have to have all three.

00:08:04.068 --> 00:08:06.439
I became obsessed with
this thing called the why.

00:08:06.439 --> 00:08:08.838
I figured out how to find
my why, and it restored

00:08:08.838 --> 00:08:12.449
my passion to levels I had
never experienced before.

00:08:12.449 --> 00:08:14.867
And more importantly, I
figured out how to help others

00:08:14.867 --> 00:08:17.568
find theirs, and I did
what anyone would do.

00:08:17.568 --> 00:08:19.240
When you discover something
beautiful, you share it

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:21.282
with your friends, and
my friends started making

00:08:21.282 --> 00:08:23.309
crazy life changes themselves.

00:08:23.309 --> 00:08:24.889
And they started finding
happiness and passion

00:08:24.889 --> 00:08:27.288
that they'd never experienced before.

00:08:27.288 --> 00:08:29.514
It was me solving my own
problem that happened

00:08:29.514 --> 00:08:31.876
to help others solve it for them, too.

00:08:31.876 --> 00:08:34.936
- When Simon Sinek found his
why, he started helping friends

00:08:34.936 --> 00:08:38.191
and friends of friends
find their whys as well.

00:08:38.191 --> 00:08:41.459
But it wasn't until his
extremely popular TEDx talk,

00:08:41.459 --> 00:08:46.191
Start With Why, which has
over 32 million views to date,

00:08:46.191 --> 00:08:49.285
that launched his career into
becoming a bestselling author,

00:08:49.285 --> 00:08:52.923
as well as a consultant to major
companies around the world.

00:08:52.923 --> 00:08:54.974
- But if you don't know
why you do what you do,

00:08:54.974 --> 00:08:58.260
and people respond to you do what you do,

00:08:58.260 --> 00:09:00.353
then how will anybody, how
will you ever get people

00:09:00.353 --> 00:09:03.148
to vote for you or buy something for you,

00:09:03.148 --> 00:09:06.280
or more importantly, be loyal,
and want to be a part of

00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:08.678
what it is that you do?

00:09:08.678 --> 00:09:10.916
Again, the goal is not just
to sell to people who need

00:09:10.916 --> 00:09:12.820
what you have, the goal
is to sell to people

00:09:12.820 --> 00:09:14.669
who believe what you believe.

00:09:14.669 --> 00:09:17.407
- What Simon Sinek taught
me is that I need to start

00:09:17.407 --> 00:09:19.261
with my why.

00:09:19.261 --> 00:09:23.748
And my why is to inspire
people to be extraordinary.

00:09:23.748 --> 00:09:27.280
So, going forward, all
the meetings I have,

00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:30.373
all the interactions I have,
all the phone calls I have,

00:09:30.373 --> 00:09:32.982
I'm going to start with my why.

00:09:32.982 --> 00:09:34.995
So, over the next few weeks,
I'm going to be taking on

00:09:34.995 --> 00:09:37.980
different challenges based
on the lessons that I learned

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:39.953
from Tony, Brene and Simon.

00:09:39.953 --> 00:09:43.969
But, Evan just gave me
a surprise challenge.

00:09:43.969 --> 00:09:46.815
I'm going to participate
is my first corporate gig,

00:09:46.815 --> 00:09:50.868
which means that all of
you are going to come

00:09:50.868 --> 00:09:54.551
behind the scenes in my
first corporate event.

00:09:54.551 --> 00:09:57.338
So the quesh of the day
is, what is that one lesson

00:09:57.338 --> 00:09:59.788
you learned from someone you look up to?

00:09:59.788 --> 00:10:03.419
It could be an entrepreneur
that you saw on Evan's channel,

00:10:03.419 --> 00:10:05.873
it could be a teacher, it
could be a family member.

00:10:05.873 --> 00:10:08.346
I would love to know what that lesson is.

00:10:08.346 --> 00:10:09.819
Please leave it in the comments below,

00:10:09.819 --> 00:10:12.080
and I will join in on the discussion.

00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:14.106
So, I want to thank you guys
for watching, and I actually

00:10:14.106 --> 00:10:17.022
have a special shout-out for Pavo Luevano.

00:10:17.022 --> 00:10:20.931
So, Pavo wrote in my last
episode that this editing

00:10:20.931 --> 00:10:24.807
